developers.

00:18:34.970 --> 00:18:38.390
At its core, RDMA gives
you an alternative path,

00:18:38.390 --> 00:18:40.250
one that's far
less CPU intensive

00:18:40.250 --> 00:18:43.100
than traditional networking,
things you'd normally handle

00:18:43.100 --> 00:18:46.160
in software message
packetization and reassembly,

00:18:46.160 --> 00:18:49.940
reliability, flow control,
congestion control,

00:18:49.940 --> 00:18:52.460
and all of that gets
offloaded to the NIC.

00:18:52.460 --> 00:18:55.100
That means your applications
can bypass the traditional

00:18:55.100 --> 00:18:57.570
networking overhead entirely.

00:18:57.570 --> 00:19:00.600
And the result is a significant
reduction in both latency

00:19:00.600 --> 00:19:02.850
and jitter, because the
heavy lifting moves

00:19:02.850 --> 00:19:05.450
out of software
and into hardware.

00:19:05.450 --> 00:19:07.610
This becomes especially
important for workloads

00:19:07.610 --> 00:19:11.540
that demand both high bandwidth
and very consistent latency,

00:19:11.540 --> 00:19:17.250
